# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Flutter Chat App — environment variable reference
# Copy this file to .env (never commit .env)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Groq LLM ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Get your key from https://console.groq.com/keys
GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Optional — override the default model
# GROQ_MODEL=llama-3.3-70b-versatile

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOW THE API KEY IS USED
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Option A — Flutter client (development / quick start)
#   Pass at build time so the app calls Groq directly:
#     flutter run --dart-define=GROQ_API_KEY=$GROQ_API_KEY
#     flutter build apk --dart-define=GROQ_API_KEY=$GROQ_API_KEY
#
#   ⚠ The key is embedded in the compiled binary. Fine for personal
#     dev use; NOT suitable for public apps.
#
# Option B — Firebase Cloud Function (recommended for production)
#   Store the key as a Firebase secret — it never leaves the server:
#     firebase functions:secrets:set GROQ_API_KEY
#   Then deploy the function:
#     cd functions && npm install && firebase deploy --only functions
#   The Flutter app writes user messages to Firestore; the function
#   reacts, calls Groq, and writes the reply back automatically.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
